WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.400
Which actor who passed away in 2021 was a key&nbsp;
player on "Pee Wee's Playhouse"? And who inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.560
a whole new generation of actors? Keep watching&nbsp;
for those and more acting greats we lost in 2021.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:20.000
Actor John Paragon died in April 2021, at 66.&nbsp;
He was best known for playing Jambi the Genie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.720
on "Pee-wee's Playhouse." Paragon met series&nbsp;
star Paul Reubens through the Groundlings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.320
the renowned improv troupe that&nbsp;
launched Will Ferrell, Kristin Wiig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.800
and Melissa McCarthy on the path to Hollywood&nbsp;
stardom. Reubens recalled on Instagram,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.920
"[John] was sly and wickedly funny.&nbsp;
We became best friends instantly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.840
In a group of exceptionally talented&nbsp;
and funny people, he stuck out."

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.520
On top of playing the disembodied genie, Paragon&nbsp;
also provided the voice of Pterri the Pterodactyl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.560
and was also a writer and director on several&nbsp;
"Playhouse" episodes. He appeared in a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.080
of other hit TV shows over the years — most&nbsp;
notably, "Seinfeld." His character Cedric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.960
was a thorn in the side of Kramer in three&nbsp;
memorable episodes including "The Soup Nazi."

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.000
Actor Frank Bonner, best known for playing&nbsp;
the spectacularly dressed Herb Tarlek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:15.600
on the classic sitcom "WKRP in Cincinnati,"&nbsp;
died in June 2021, according to TMZ. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.360
79. His daughter broke the news, posting&nbsp;
on the official "WKRP" Facebook page,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.520
"He loved his fans and was still signing&nbsp;
autograph requests up until the last few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.400
weeks of his illness. Thank you to all who&nbsp;
followed his career. He will be forever missed."

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.760
"I love you…I really do."

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.360
Bonner made his first onscreen appearance&nbsp;
in the 1967 horror film "The Equinox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.520
...A Journey Into the Supernatural."&nbsp;
But he was best known for his TV work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.040
After early appearances in shows&nbsp;
like "Mannix" and "The F.B.I.,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.760
Bonner landed the career-defining role of Herb&nbsp;
Tarlek. He portrayed the character in all but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.880
two episodes of the series. His co-star&nbsp;
Loni Anderson told the Associated Press,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.920
"He was one of the funniest men I&nbsp;
had the pleasure of working with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.000
and he was the nicest man I have ever known."

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.240
Actor Joanne Linville played a Romulan&nbsp;
[RAHM-you-luhn] commander in "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.200
The Original Series" and went on to appear in&nbsp;
the likes of "Gunsmoke," "Barnaby Jones" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:17.600
"Hawaii Five-O." She died in June 2021,&nbsp;
at 93. Her family shared in a statement,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.640
"[She] lived a full life.&nbsp;
[She was] one whose spirit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.880
passion for art and life was an inspiration&nbsp;
to all who had the pleasure of knowing her."

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.480
Linville made her debut in the '50s with&nbsp;
movies like "The Goddess" and "Copper Canyon."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.280
But she was perhaps best known for her memorable&nbsp;
guest role in "The Enterprise Incident," in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.240
her Romulan commander captures Captain Kirk and&nbsp;
Mr. Spock — falling in love with the latter.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.240
"It will be our secret."

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.520
British actor Jackie Lane – best known&nbsp;
for playing the Doctor's companion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:54.880
Dodo Chaplet, in the long-running sci-fi series&nbsp;
"Doctor Who" — died in June 2021. She was 79.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.280
After getting her start in local&nbsp;
theater as a teenager, by the 1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.080
Lane had secured regular theater work.&nbsp;
She moved to London to pursue a TV career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.080
and landed a role on the BBC's&nbsp;
short-lived soap opera "Compact."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.520
Having appeared in 19 episodes of "Doctor Who,"&nbsp;
she was then let go from the series. Disheartened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.400
Lane left the industry for years, but kept her&nbsp;
ear to the ground as an agent for voice actors.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.320
Actor and renowned drama coach&nbsp;
Ann Ratray died in June 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.840
at 81. Her son, "Home Alone" star Devin Ratray,&nbsp;
confirmed the news on Instagram, writing,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.520
"She was in her bed surrounded by&nbsp;
her family. […] The family requests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.400
privacy […] and appreciates your sentiments."

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:42.000
Starting out in the industry as a Miss America&nbsp;
contestant in 1958, she starred on Broadway and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.360
landed a few small TV and movie roles in the&nbsp;
'70s. But Ratray would become best known for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.200
her mentoring in later years. She prepared her son&nbsp;
for his turn as Buzz McCallister in "Home Alone"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.640
and was influential in the career of "Catch-22"&nbsp;
star Graham Patrick Martin, who tweeted,

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.920
"Ann Ratray is the reason I am&nbsp;
an actor. Forever grateful."

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.160
Bulgarian singer and actor Lorina Kamburova,&nbsp;
who featured in a number of horror movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.440
died of pneumonia in May 2021. The&nbsp;
condition was caused by COVID-19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.600
according to the Russian outlet,&nbsp;
Gazeta. She was just 30 years old.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.720
Her career began in her native country,&nbsp;
with the series "Liaisons" in 2015.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.600
Two years later, she appeared in three horror&nbsp;
films, including a prequel to Tobe Hooper's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.560
"The Texas Chain Saw Massacre"; 2017's&nbsp;
"Leatherface." The final product didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.000
fare all that well with critics,&nbsp;
but the Los Angeles Times noted,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.720
"[It's] the best 'Chainsaw' movie that doesn't&nbsp;
have 'directed by Tobe Hooper' in the credits."

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.160
Lorina landed a spot in 2017's "Nightworld," and&nbsp;
then "Day of the Dead: Bloodline." In what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.120
become one of her final roles, the actor played&nbsp;
Dr. Sandy Peterson in "Doom: Annihilation."

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.800
Soap star Stuart Damon, who played Dr.&nbsp;
Alan Quartermaine in over 300 episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:04.320
of "General Hospital," died in June 2021. He&nbsp;
was 84 years old. His son recalled to KABC,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.160
"['General Hospital'] was his favorite&nbsp;
place to be. He loved playing Alan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.560
and was always so appreciative of that&nbsp;
role and that job. It was his passion."

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.920
Having gotten his start in theater, Damon&nbsp;
launched his TV career in the U.K. on shows like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.480
"The Saint," "The New Avengers," and "The&nbsp;
Champions." He worked out of London for over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.920
a decade, but trekked back to the States for&nbsp;
the iconic soap. He said in a 2010 interview,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.280
"I was thinking 'General&nbsp;
Hospital' might be a 13-week gig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.920
and 31 years later, I was still there."

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.520
His character was killed off in 2007, but he&nbsp;
continued to return in flashbacks. The soap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.360
veteran went on to appear in "As the&nbsp;
World Turns" and "Days of Our Lives."

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:50.560
Model and actor Daniel Mickelson, died&nbsp;
at just 23 years old, on July 4th, 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.600
He was best known for his&nbsp;
role on Brat TV's "Mani."

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.120
"There's no time like the present."

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.920
His sister, model Meredith Mickelson,&nbsp;
revealed the news on Instagram, writing,

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.280
"My heart is shattered and to write this&nbsp;
feels so wrong. Yesterday, I lost my brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.240
best friend, and the other half of my heart.&nbsp;
There wasn't a person I loved more on this Earth."

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:16.080
Mickelson made his acting debut in 2019, playing&nbsp;
con man Billy on the "Mani" web series. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.440
Atlanta-born up-and-comer landed his first film&nbsp;
role soon after, appearing in the indie horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:26.880
"The Killer Clown Meets the Candy Man." He also&nbsp;
reportedly was just beginning his modeling career.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:32.320
Meredith's tribute post was quickly flooded&nbsp;
with heartfelt messages from other famous faces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.280
Jordyn Woods sent her prayers, while Paris Hilton&nbsp;
wrote of her sadness about the news. Among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.320
most heartbreaking tributes was the one made by&nbsp;
Daniel's girlfriend, Maddie Haley. She wrote,

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:46.640
"I wish my plans to spend the rest of my life&nbsp;
with you weren't stripped away from me like this."

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.760
Filmmaker, director and actor Robert&nbsp;
Downey Sr. — father of Robert Downey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.640
Jr. — died of Parkinson's in July 2021. He was 85.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.160
The New York native enlisted in the&nbsp;
Army when he was still in his teens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:05.120
and would land in minor league baseball, before&nbsp;
writing his first play. After cutting his teeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:10.080
with Off-off-Broadway productions, he&nbsp;
made the low budget feature "Babo 73."

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.160
While his films were never box office draws,&nbsp;
the critically-acclaimed works would inspire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.920
a generation of filmmakers.&nbsp;
One was Paul Thomas Anderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:23.280
who went on to cast Downey Sr. in 1997's&nbsp;
"Boogie Nights." His best known film as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:29.840
director was 1969's "Putney Swope." It was added&nbsp;
to the U.S. National Film Registry, in 2016.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:34.800
Veteran actor Suzzanne Douglas&nbsp;
died of cancer in July 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.720
at 64. She starred in the '90s sitcom "The Parent&nbsp;
'Hood" and later appeared in Ava DuVernay's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.840
critically acclaimed drama "When They See Us."&nbsp;
DuVernay described her in a tweet, writing,

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.120
"[Suzzanne was] a quiet, elegant force. A&nbsp;
gentlewoman. A gem of a lady. A confident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.800
caring actor who breathed life into&nbsp;
the words and made them shimmer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:57.600
I'm grateful that our paths in this life crossed."

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.920
Douglas grew up in a "rough" part of&nbsp;
Chicago, and went on to study theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.280
at Illinois State University, alongside the&nbsp;
likes of John Malkovich and Gary Sinise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:13.600
Douglas made her on-screen debut in 1981 and would&nbsp;
become a sitcom mainstay the following decade.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.120
In what was her best known role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:21.280
Douglas played matriarch Jerri Peterson&nbsp;
on "The Parent 'Hood" from 1995 to 1999.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.360
Texas native Charles Robinson —&nbsp;
best known for playing court clerk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.880
Mac on the sitcom "Night Court"&nbsp;
— died in July 2021. He was 75.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.400
Robinson made his big screen debut in&nbsp;
Jack Nicholson's 1971 directorial debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.720
"Drive, He Said." And by the early&nbsp;
'80s, he was landing recurring TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:46.000
roles in shows like "Buffalo Bill." But&nbsp;
in 1984, he took his career-defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.400
turn as Mac the popular clerk on "Night&nbsp;
Court." He once said in an interview,

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.480
"Most people really liked Mac because Mac&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.040
kinda kept a lid over the pot. I will live&nbsp;
with 'Night Court' for the rest of my life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.080
I could do films, theater, television&nbsp;
forever, and it's always 'Night Court.'"

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.760
One of his final projects was&nbsp;
Freeform's "Love in the Time of Corona,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.080
in which his wife co-starred as his nurse.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.360
"Cheers to you, my dear."

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:18.480
According to the Hollywood Reporter, actor Robert&nbsp;
Sacchi died in June 2021. He was 89. Sacchi looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.120
so much like Humphrey Bogart that he once starred&nbsp;
in a film called "The Man With Bogart's Face."

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.640
"Didn't I see you sometime on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:27.840
television in old movies?"
"No, that's somebody else."&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.640
"You sure look like you."
"No, I look like him."

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:35.280
While Sacchi made an appearance in a music&nbsp;
video for Phil Collins' song "I Wish It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:41.040
Would Rain Down," he was primarily known for&nbsp;
comedy. The 1999 rom-com "Blast from the Past"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.400
became his final role. But fans&nbsp;
of action flicks will no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.160
recognize Sacchi as a control&nbsp;
tower engineer in "Die Hard 2."

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:55.360
Martial arts movie icon "Sonny" Chiba died in&nbsp;
August 2021 of complications from Covid. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.960
was 82. He made his international debut&nbsp;
in the 1970s with "The Street Fighter"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.280
and later appeared in "Kill Bill" and&nbsp;
"The Fast and the Furious: Tokyo Drift."

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.600
A black belt in numerous disciplines, Chiba&nbsp;
was the real deal when it came to martial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.240
arts. His first crossover success&nbsp;
was 1974's "The Street Fighter,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:19.360
the dub of which became a cult favorite in the&nbsp;
U.S. The film would inspire Quentin Tarantino,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:24.640
who referenced it in his script for 1993's&nbsp;
"True Romance." Chiba passed away before his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:29.600
final feature film — the gangster movie,&nbsp;
"Bond of Justice: Kizuna" — was released.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.800
TV legend Ed Asner , best known for playing&nbsp;
news producer Lou Grant on both "The Mary Tyler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:42.400
Moore Show" and the series "Lou Grant," died in&nbsp;
August 2021, at 91. His family shared in a tweet,

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.800
"We are sorry to say that our beloved&nbsp;
patriarch passed away this morning peacefully.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:52.480
Words cannot express the sadness we feel. With a&nbsp;
kiss on your head — Goodnight, dad. We love you."

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.760
"You've got spunk."&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.160
"Well, yes…"
"I hate spunk!"

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:02.880
After starring on Broadway, and in film and TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.840
Asner hit the big time when he was cast as&nbsp;
cranky Lou Grant on "The Mary Tyler Moore Show,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:12.080
a role that would provide him the chance to&nbsp;
flex his dramatic muscles in the spinoff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:17.360
"Lou Grant." Across both shows, the role&nbsp;
would net Asner five of his seven Emmys.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:21.920
Of course, younger audiences probably&nbsp;
know Asner best as Santa Claus from "Elf,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.960
and as the voice of Carl Fredricksen in "Up."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:30.880
Asner was inducted into the Academy of&nbsp;
Television Arts &amp; Sciences Hall of Fame in 1996.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:36.080
Emmy-nominated actor Michael K. Williams&nbsp;
was found dead in his New York apartment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:41.840
on September 6, 2021. He was 54. Best&nbsp;
known for his spellbinding turns on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:46.240
numerous HBO shows and movies, a rep&nbsp;
for the network said in a statement,

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.840
"[He was] a member of the HBO&nbsp;
family for more than 20 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.880
While the world is aware of his&nbsp;
immense talents as an artist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.360
we knew Michael as a dear friend who was beloved&nbsp;
by all who had the privilege to work with him."

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.080
"On a personal level, I truly believe&nbsp;
that all my characters, they choose me."

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:06.960
Williams worked as a background dancer for&nbsp;
Madonna, Crystal Waters, and George Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.160
before switching his focus to acting. He was&nbsp;
soon spotted by none other than Tupac Shakur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:17.760
who pushed for him to be cast in the 1996 film&nbsp;
"Bullet." Williams would go on to play a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:22.320
of gangster roles in the years that followed.&nbsp;
But his portrayal of openly gay stick-up man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.680
Omar Little in "The Wire" garnered widespread&nbsp;
praise. The actor told the New York Times,

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:31.680
"Omar definitely helped soften the&nbsp;
blow of homophobia in my community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.120
and it opened up a dialogue."

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.960
His most recent work was as Montrose&nbsp;
Freeman in "Lovecraft Country,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:41.520
which earned the versatile star his&nbsp;
fifth and, ultimately, final Emmy nod.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:48.880
Comedian and actor Norm Macdonald died of cancer&nbsp;
in September 2021. He was 61. The TV icon starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:53.600
in films including "Dirty Work" and "Screwed"&nbsp;
but was best known for his time on "Saturday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:59.200
Night Live." Macdonald kept his diagnosis secret&nbsp;
for nine years. As a friend of his told Deadline,

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:03.520
"[He didn't want it to] affect the way the&nbsp;
audience or any of his loved ones saw him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:08.080
Norm was a pure comic. He once wrote that&nbsp;
'a joke should catch someone by surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:11.040
it should never pander.' He&nbsp;
certainly never pandered."

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:17.120
A 1990 appearance on "Star Search" led to TV&nbsp;
writing gigs, and McDonald soon found himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:23.280
penning gags for "Roseanne." But in 1993, NBC&nbsp;
came calling, and MacDonald began a five-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:28.080
stint on "SNL" that's remembered chiefly&nbsp;
for his time fronting "Weekend Update."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:33.600
His turn as anchor was defined by his relentless&nbsp;
criticism of O.J. Simpson and his murder trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:37.280
which some say contributed to his&nbsp;
axing from the show. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:42.480
MacDonald himself believed that his humor was just&nbsp;
too out there. As he once told The New York Times,

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.400
"We were doing experimental stuff."

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:48.640
Macdonald went on to star on "The Norm&nbsp;
Show" and worked on several movies with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:53.280
former "SNL" cast members; most notably&nbsp;
Adam Sandler's "Billy Madison," Eddie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.280 --> 00:14:01.840
Murphy's "Dr. Dolittle" — as Lucky the&nbsp;
dog — and Rob Schneider's "The Animal."

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:02.560
"And that's the way it is,&nbsp;
folks. Goodnight and good luck."

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:07.200
Actor James Michael Tyler was known all&nbsp;
around the world for his portrayal of Gunther,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:10.640
the eccentric and shy manager&nbsp;
of Central Perk on "Friends."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:17.360
He died of prostate cancer in October&nbsp;
2021, at 59. His rep shared in a statement,

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:22.000
"The world knew him as Gunther (the seventh&nbsp;
'Friend')…but Michael's loved ones knew him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:25.120
as an actor, musician, cancer-awareness advocate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:28.640
and loving husband. If you met him&nbsp;
once you made a friend for life."

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:32.960
His character's crush on Jennifer Aniston's&nbsp;
Rachel Greene became a running gag over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:36.320
course of 10 seasons. And it&nbsp;
was a remarkable storyline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.640
when you consider that Gunther wasn't even&nbsp;
supposed to be a fully-realized character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:45.840
Tyler was originally cast as a background&nbsp;
actor, but "Friends" co-creators Marta Kauffman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:49.680
and David Crane saw something&nbsp;
more. They said in a statement,

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.600
"His unique spirit caught our eye and&nbsp;
we knew we had to make him a character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:56.800
He made Gunther's unrequited&nbsp;
love incredibly relatable."

00:14:56.800 --> 00:15:01.040
He went on to appear in several other hit&nbsp;
series, but he'll always be remembered as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:05.760
part of the "Friends" family. Jennifer Aniston&nbsp;
took to Instagram to write in Tyler's memory,

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.640
"'Friends' would not have been the&nbsp;
same without you. Thank you for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.640
laughter you brought to the show and to&nbsp;
all of our lives. You will be so missed."

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:20.160
Comedian and actor Anthony "AJ" Johnson died&nbsp;
in September 2021, at 55. He was best known for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:25.680
his standout turn as Ezal in the classic comedy&nbsp;
"Friday." As his rep told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.280
"We lost an icon. He has left with us amazing&nbsp;
memories of his laughter, dynamic acting skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:34.720
but most of all his enormous&nbsp;
personality and heart of gold."

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:38.720
Co-writer and co-star of the&nbsp;
stoner comedy, Ice Cube, tweeted,

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:42.640
"[He was a] naturally funny dude who was&nbsp;
straight outta Compton at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:46.800
Sorry I couldn't bring your character Ezal&nbsp;
back to the big screen in 'Last Friday.'"

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:52.240
According to Johnson, he was supposed to reprise&nbsp;
the role in 2000's "Next Friday," but Cube cut him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:57.600
out from the movie to save money. He told Vlad&nbsp;
TV that he and Cube "shook hands" on a figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:02.240
only for Cube to hire someone else and begin&nbsp;
shooting early — without telling Johnson.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:05.520
"If you made it out of Compton,&nbsp;
you can make it anywhere."

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:11.360
Character actor Dean Stockwell&nbsp;
died in November 2021. He was 85.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:16.880
Stockwell appeared alongside the likes of Errol&nbsp;
Flynn, Frank Sinatra and Gene Kelly as a child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:21.520
And after falling out of love with acting,&nbsp;
he experienced a career revival in the '80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:25.840
appearing in "Paris, Texas," as well as&nbsp;
the David Lynch films "Dune" and "Blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:30.240
Velvet." He also played a crime boss in&nbsp;
Jonathan Demme's "Married to the Mob,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:33.200
which led to an Oscar nom&nbsp;
for Best Supporting Actor.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:37.440
He became beloved by sci-fi fans for&nbsp;
his turn as cigar-munching ladies' man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:42.960
Al Calavicci on "Quantum Leap,"&nbsp;
from 1989 to 1993. Scott Bakula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:46.880
who starred on the show, paid tribute&nbsp;
to Stockwell in a statement, recalling,

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:51.200
"Having been a famous child actor, he had&nbsp;
a soft spot for every young actor who came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:56.320
on our set. I loved him dearly and was honored&nbsp;
to know him. He made me a better human being."

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:58.560
"I'm outta here."

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:02.480
Stockwell went on to land roles&nbsp;
on "Stargate SG-1," "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:04.640
Enterprise," and "Battlestar Galactica."

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:10.320
Character actor Art LaFleur was best known&nbsp;
for his turn as baseball legend Babe Ruth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:17.840
in the 1993 film "The Sandlot." He died in&nbsp;
November 2021, at age 78, of Parkinson's.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:20.320
His wife, Shelley, confirmed on Facebook,

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:24.240
"He was a generous and selfless man,&nbsp;
which carried over to his acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:27.360
but more importantly, it was who&nbsp;
he was for his family and friends."

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:32.560
LaFleur originally moved to California&nbsp;
in 1975 to become a screenwriter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:37.520
He went on to land roles in 1989's&nbsp;
"Field of Dreams" and 1992's "Mr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:42.320
Baseball," but fans never forgot "The&nbsp;
Sandlot." LaFleur once told Media Mikes,

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:43.760
"When I went into the audition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:48.320
I went in 'as' Babe. […] I went in with&nbsp;
a cigar…the Babe always had a cigar."

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:52.400
Actor Joey Morgan died in November 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:57.200
He was just 28. Best known for his turn&nbsp;
as Augie Foster in the zombie comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:01.760
"Scouts Guide to the Zombie Apocalypse,"&nbsp;
his rep confirmed to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:06.640
"It was a shock and devastated all of us&nbsp;
who loved him. He will be dearly missed."

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:11.200
"Scouts Guide to the Zombie Apocalypse" was&nbsp;
Morgan's acting debut. He went on to feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:16.240
in a number of other teen comedies, playing a&nbsp;
hacker in "Compadres " and later appearing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:22.560
"Sierra Burgess Is a Loser" and "Camp Manna." He&nbsp;
returned to the big screen in 2020, co-starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:27.920
in the critically acclaimed sci-fi adventure&nbsp;
film "Max Reload and the Nether Blasters."

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:32.480
Actor Heath Freeman was best known for&nbsp;
playing serial killer Howard Epps on "Bones,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:35.760
as well as turns on "NCIS" and "Raising the Bar."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:40.640
He died in 2021 at the age of&nbsp;
41. His reps said in a statement,

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:43.280
"[We are] truly devastated at the loss. [He was a]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.640
brilliant human being with an&nbsp;
intense and soulful spirit."

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:51.040
Freeman made his small screen&nbsp;
debut in a 2001 episode of "ER."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:57.360
And in 2003, he landed on "NCIS." But&nbsp;
by 2005, he made his first appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:02.800
as Howard Epps on Season 1 of "Bones."&nbsp;
Freeman appeared in three features in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:06.480
including the Luke Wilson-led&nbsp;
football flick, "12 Mighty Orphans."

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:11.120
New York-born actor Yaphet Kotto,&nbsp;
who appeared opposite Roger Moore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:15.120
as a corrupt Caribbean politician in&nbsp;
the James Bond film Live and Let Die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:21.280
and later played engineer Dennis Parker in&nbsp;
Ridley Scott's 1979 sci-fi flick, Alien – died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:27.200
in the Philippines in March 2021. His wife&nbsp;
confirmed the news in a Facebook post writing,

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:31.040
"You played a villain on some of your&nbsp;
movies but for me you're a real hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:35.360
A good man, a good father, a good&nbsp;
husband and a decent human being."

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.720
He was 81.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:40.720
Kotto made his movie debut&nbsp;
aged 23 in Nothing but a Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:46.160
a film about a Black couple dealing with&nbsp;
racial discrimination in 1960s Alabama. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:51.200
sheer size led to him being cast in a variety&nbsp;
of hardman roles in the years that followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:54.640
something that always bothered him.&nbsp;
He once told The Baltimore Sun,

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:57.520
"I want to try to play a much&nbsp;
more sensitive man. There is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:00.880
an aspect of Black people's lives&nbsp;
that is not running or jumping."

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:04.800
Other notable credits include the&nbsp;
Stephen King adaptation The Running Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:08.800
in which Kotto fought for his freedom&nbsp;
alongside Arnold Schwarzenegger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.720
and the '90s police procedural&nbsp;
series Homicide: Life on the Street.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:18.160
Award-winning actress Jessica Walter, who&nbsp;
won an Emmy for her turn as a San Francisco&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:23.200
cop in Ironside spinoff Amy Prentiss, and&nbsp;
became known to later generations for playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:27.680
Lucille Bluth in Arrested Development&nbsp;
and voicing Malory Archer in Archer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:32.880
died in her sleep in March 2021. Daughter&nbsp;
Brooke Bowman said in a statement,

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:37.520
"A working actor for over six decades, her&nbsp;
greatest pleasure was bringing joy to others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:42.480
through her storytelling both on screen and off.&nbsp;
While her legacy will live on through her body of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:48.160
work, she will also be remembered by many for&nbsp;
her wit, class, and overall joie de vivre."

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:49.760
She was 80 years old.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:54.640
The New York-born Walter began her career on&nbsp;
Broadway and quickly started turning heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:59.040
picking up the Clarence Derwent Award for Most&nbsp;
Promising Newcomer for her work in the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:03.440
Photo Finish. She continued to impress&nbsp;
after making the move to the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:07.360
where Walter played Clint Eastwood's&nbsp;
knife-wielding ex-girlfriend in the actor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:14.080
directorial debut, 1971's Play Misty for Me,&nbsp;
earning a Golden Globe nomination in the process.

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:19.280
She was up for multiple Emmys over the course of&nbsp;
her career, including for Arrested Development.

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:22.800
"I don't have the milk of&nbsp;
mother's kindness in me anymore."

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:27.040
"Yeah, that udder's been dry for a&nbsp;
while though, hasn't it? Kind of."

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:33.600
British character actor Paul Ritter, who won&nbsp;
praise for his powerful performance in HBO's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:40.640
Golden Globe-winning miniseries Chernobyl, died&nbsp;
of a brain tumor in April 2021 at the age of 54.

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:45.280
Ritter was well known in theater communities&nbsp;
on both sides of the Atlantic, having been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:50.240
nominated for both an Olivier Award and&nbsp;
a Tony Award in his time. He made his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:55.520
on-screen bow in 1992, with an appearance in&nbsp;
the long-running police procedural The Bill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.520 --> 00:22:01.120
He went on to carve out a career as a prolific&nbsp;
TV actor. Alongside his appearance in Chernobyl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:05.120
his best-known performance came in the&nbsp;
British sitcom Friday Night Dinner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:10.160
where he played oddball dad Martin Goodman.&nbsp;
Robert Popper, creator of Fright Night Dinner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:14.480
said that he was "devastated at this&nbsp;
terribly sad news" in a tribute tweet.

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:17.600
"Paul was a lovely, wonderful human being. Kind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:21.200
funny, super caring and the&nbsp;
greatest actor I ever worked with."

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:28.000
Grammy-nominated rapper and actor DMX, whose&nbsp;
songs regularly broke the Billboard Hot 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:34.160
in the late '90s and early '00s, died after being&nbsp;
hospitalized in April 2021. He was rushed to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:40.080
critical care unit following a drug overdose,&nbsp;
but ultimately passed away at the age of 50.

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:45.440
Born Earl Simmons in Mount Vernon, New York, DMX&nbsp;
spent much of his childhood in the care system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:50.320
after being removed from his physically abusive&nbsp;
mother. He began developing his gritty stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:55.360
persona, "Dark Man X," during his time, and he&nbsp;
remained true to his name as his peers started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.360 --> 00:23:01.200
to gravitate towards designer labels and smoother&nbsp;
beats. Songs like "What's My Name" and "Party Up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:06.080
(Up in Here)" had made him a star come the turn of&nbsp;
the millennium, and Hollywood soon came calling.

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:09.520
DMX appeared in a string of&nbsp;
action movies around this time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:14.400
For example, there was 2000's Romeo Must&nbsp;
Die, a martial arts-heavy Shakespeare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:20.240
transplant starring Jet Li and Aaliyah. And&nbsp;
then, DMX headlined in 2001's Exit Wounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:25.760
and 2003's Cradle 2 the Grave, alongside&nbsp;
Steven Seagal and Jet Li, respectively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:30.080
He never walked away from acting, and he was still&nbsp;
active in the industry at the time of his death.

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:36.000
British actress Helen McCrory, best known for&nbsp;
her turns as Narcissa Malfoy in the Harry Potter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:43.440
movies and Aunt Polly in all five series of&nbsp;
Peaky Blinders, died in April 2021. She was 52.

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:48.800
McCrory was born in London but moved to Cameroon&nbsp;
and then Tanzania with her diplomat dad when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:54.000
was still just a toddler. She returned to the&nbsp;
UK for her studies, and after a year in Italy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.000 --> 00:24:00.560
she enrolled at Drama Centre London. She made her&nbsp;
TV debut in 1993 and appeared in her first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:03.920
the Kate Beckinsale-led thriller&nbsp;
Uncovered, the following year.

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:08.560
She went on to appear in films like The Count&nbsp;
of Monte Cristo, Helen Mirren's The Queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:13.120
and the James Bond installment Skyfall, but&nbsp;
she never lost touch with her theater roots.

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:18.720
Olympia Dukakis – the star of the stage&nbsp;
and screen who won an Oscar for her turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:24.160
as Cher's meddling mother in hit '80s&nbsp;
rom-com "Moonstruck" — died in May 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:27.760
her brother confirmed in a&nbsp;
Facebook post. She was 89.

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:33.040
The daughter of Greek immigrants, Dukakis was born&nbsp;
in Massachusetts and attended Boston University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:38.080
where she mastered in performing arts. She met&nbsp;
actor-producer Louis Zorich after moving to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:44.480
New York, and they would tie the knot in 1962,&nbsp;
the year she made her debut. By the mid-1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:50.000
she had two Obie Awards to her name and was well&nbsp;
on her way to becoming a respected Broadway star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.520
though she'll likely be remembered&nbsp;
by most for her work on the screen.

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:58.800
Dukakis was named Best Supporting Actress at both&nbsp;
the Academy Awards and the Golden Globes for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:04.480
performance as Rose Castorini in "Moonstruck."&nbsp;
Sally Field, who worked with Dukakis on "Steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:09.360
Magnolias," called her, quote, "unique and&nbsp;
talented and one of a kind" in her own tribute.

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:14.640
Beloved Hollywood veteran Norman Lloyd, who was&nbsp;
one of the oldest living actors in the business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:18.320
died in May 2021 at the age 106.

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:23.760
The New Jersey-born, Brooklyn-raised Lloyd got&nbsp;
his first break back in 1932 when he was a student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:28.720
at New York University. He would eventually end&nbsp;
up working with Orson Welles, playing Cinna the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:33.680
Poet in Welles' famous adaptation of "Julius&nbsp;
Caesar." His first film — Alfred Hitchcock's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:38.400
"Saboteur" — soon followed. The hit spy thriller&nbsp;
was the beginning of a long-lasting professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:44.640
relationship between Hitchcock and Lloyd, who&nbsp;
went on to produce and/or direct over 250 episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:49.680
of "Alfred Hitchcock Presents" and "The&nbsp;
Alfred Hitchcock Hour" in the '50s and '60s.

00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:55.520
Lloyd was nominated for a Primetime Emmy for his&nbsp;
work on the TV movie "Steambath" in the '70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.520 --> 00:26:00.800
and in the following decade, he became known&nbsp;
for NBC medical drama "St. Elsewhere," appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:07.360
as Dr. Daniel Auschlander between 1982 and 1988.&nbsp;
Other notable roles include Peter Weir's "Dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:12.640
Poets Society," Martin Scorsese's "The Age of&nbsp;
Innocence," and Charlie Chaplin's "Limelight."

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:17.680
Comedian and actor Paul Mooney – who&nbsp;
regularly collaborated with Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:22.480
Pryor in the 1970s and '80s and later&nbsp;
became known for his work on "In Living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:28.400
Color" and "Chappelle's Show" — died of&nbsp;
a heart attack in May 2021. He was 79.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:33.120
Mooney met Pryor at a party in the late&nbsp;
1960s, and the pair quickly hit it off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:36.320
They ended up penning an episode&nbsp;
of "Sanford and Son" together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:39.680
and would collaborate many more&nbsp;
times in the years that followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:44.720
including on the variety series "The Richard Pryor&nbsp;
Show," the stand-up film "Richard Pryor: Live on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:50.080
the Sunset Strip," and on Pryor's biographical&nbsp;
vehicle "Jo Jo Dancer, Your Life Is Calling."

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:54.400
Mooney brought his patented style of comedy&nbsp;
to Keenen Ivory Wayans' "In Living Color"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:59.120
in the '90s. And in the following decade, he&nbsp;
became a writer on another hit sketch show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:04.000
"Chappelle's Show." Mooney also appeared on-screen&nbsp;
in "Chappelle's Show," playing characters such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:08.720
a very opinionated movie critic and a&nbsp;
Nostradamus parody. Notable big-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:13.200
credits include "The Buddy Holly Story," in&nbsp;
which he played seminal singer Sam Cooke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:18.240
Spike Lee's "Bamboozled," and the Mike Epps-led&nbsp;
comedy "Meet the Blacks," Mooney's final film.

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:23.280
Former child actor Kevin Clark, who played&nbsp;
percussionist turned drummer Freddy Jones in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:28.640
the Jack Black film "School of Rock," died&nbsp;
after being hit by a motorist in May 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:33.760
Clark was riding his bicycle in his home city&nbsp;
of Chicago when he was struck by a car. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:39.840
pronounced dead at Illinois Masonic Medical&nbsp;
Center around 45 minutes later. He was 32.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:43.120
Clark landed his part in Richard&nbsp;
Linklater's "School of Rock,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:47.440
his one and only acting credit, after he&nbsp;
tagged along with a friend to the auditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:50.720
His mother, Allison Clark,&nbsp;
told the Chicago Sun-Times,

00:27:50.720 --> 00:27:55.360
"He just kind of shined. He took it on right&nbsp;
away, but he never really acted afterward."

00:27:55.360 --> 00:28:00.640
He did, however, keep the music up. Clark went on&nbsp;
to play in a number of bands in the Chicago area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:06.080
and even taught some classes. Robbie Goldberg, who&nbsp;
played with Clark in the band Robbie Gold, said,

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:08.640
"He was motivated and loved to write songs,"

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:13.200
His Hollywood career was short, but Clark was&nbsp;
a stand-out performer in "School of Rock,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:18.960
a cult favorite that spawned a Broadway show and&nbsp;
a TV adaptation. He was photographed alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:24.320
co-star Jack Black as recently as 2018,&nbsp;
and Black said in an Instagram tribute,

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:29.840
"Kevin is gone. Way too soon. Beautiful&nbsp;
soul. So many great memories. Heartbroken."

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:33.440
Two-time Tony Award nominee Samuel E. Wright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:38.160
most famous for his iconic portrayal of Sebastian&nbsp;
the crab in Disney's "The Little Mermaid,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:44.080
died of prostate cancer in May&nbsp;
2021, Deadline confirmed. He was 74.

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:48.880
The South Carolina-native began his career on&nbsp;
Broadway, appearing in the original production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:56.400
of "Jesus Christ Superstar" in 1971. He made&nbsp;
his TV debut in 1976 and went on to land a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.400 --> 00:29:02.240
in 1988's "Bird," the Forest Whitaker-led&nbsp;
biopic about jazz musician Charlie Parker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:07.200
He voiced King Triton's forthright advisor&nbsp;
Sebastian in Disney's animated classic "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:11.840
Little Mermaid" the following year, a role&nbsp;
he would reprise many times in TV spinoffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:15.680
and related Disney media. He&nbsp;
once told Entertainment Weekly,

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:20.960
"Sebastian has given me what I've always wanted&nbsp;
as an actor, and that's immortality. As far as I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:25.840
concerned, that's why I'm in the business. I want&nbsp;
the whole world to love me, remember me forever."

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:29.200
Wright got the chance to portray&nbsp;
another iconic Disney character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:34.720
when "The Lion King" came to Broadway in 1997,&nbsp;
playing the role of Simba's father, Mufasa.

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:40.080
Veteran TV actor Robert Hogan died of&nbsp;
complications from pneumonia in May&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:42.400
2021. He was 87.

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:47.760
The New York-born actor served with the U.S. Army&nbsp;
in Korea and briefly trained as an engineer upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:52.880
returning, but an aptitude test told him that he&nbsp;
was in the wrong field. Hogan secured a place at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:57.680
the American Academy of Dramatic Arts and went&nbsp;
on to become a stalwart on the small screen.

00:29:57.680 --> 00:30:01.680
For many, Hogan was best known for&nbsp;
his recurring roles on classic ABC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:06.400
shows "The Young Marrieds," "Peyton Place," and&nbsp;
"Petticoat Affair," though younger viewers will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.400 --> 00:30:11.760
no doubt remember him from his turn in HBO's&nbsp;
"The Wire." Hogan portrayed Louis Sobotka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:18.000
father and moral compass to Pablo Schreiber's Nick&nbsp;
Sobotka, in four 2002 episodes of the hit show.

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:23.280
Renowned character actor Ned Beatty, who&nbsp;
played ill-fated city boy Bobby Trippe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:31.040
in John Boorman's 1972 classic "Deliverance,"&nbsp;
died of natural causes in June 2021. He was 83.

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:35.200
A native of Kentucky, Beatty made&nbsp;
his acting debut in his late teens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:39.280
scoring a singing part in a local stage&nbsp;
production. He went on to feature in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:44.800
original production of "The Great White Hope,"&nbsp;
which moved to Broadway and ran until 1970.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:49.440
Two years later, Beatty made his memorable&nbsp;
and haunting movie debut in "Deliverance."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:54.560
He would remain best known for Boorman's film,&nbsp;
but Beatty's most celebrated performance actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.560 --> 00:31:00.400
came a few years after it — he was nominated for&nbsp;
Best Supporting Actor for his work in "Network."

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:07.520
"You have meddled with the primal forces&nbsp;
of nature, Mr. Beale, and I won't have it!"

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:11.360
Beatty would go on to work with many&nbsp;
more of Hollywood's biggest directors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:16.000
including Richard Donner, Steven Spielberg,&nbsp;
John Cassavetes, and Robert Altman.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:20.800
Ohio native Lisa Banes, who was in&nbsp;
'80s films "Cocktail" and "Young Girls"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:25.600
and later appeared in "Gone Girl" and "A Cure&nbsp;
for Wellness," died after a hit-and-run in June&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:33.280
2021. Banes was taken to Mount Sinai Morningside&nbsp;
Hospital, but she died 10 days later. She was 65.

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:37.440
Banes' career in acting began in the&nbsp;
city where her life was tragically taken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:42.560
She studied at New York's Juilliard School and&nbsp;
appeared in numerous Broadway shows. She made her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:48.880
movie debut in Tony Richardson's "The Hotel New&nbsp;
Hampshire" in 1984, and by the end of the decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.880 --> 00:31:53.680
she was sharing the screen with Tom Cruise,&nbsp;
playing wealthy barfly Bonnie in "Cocktail."

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:59.280
She worked largely in TV during the 1990s,&nbsp;
co-starring in the Emmy-nominated drama series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:04.080
"The Trials of Rosie O'Neill" before a string&nbsp;
of guest roles in the likes of "L.A. Law," "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.080 --> 00:32:09.120
Trek: Deep Space Nine," and "Frasier." Banes&nbsp;
would continue to appear regularly on American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:14.480
TV screens, with casting directors often keen&nbsp;
to take advantage of her age-defying looks.
 
 &nbsp;

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:18.960
Philadelphia-born actress Marion&nbsp;
Ramsey, best known for playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:24.640
soft spoken Officer Laverne Hooks in the&nbsp;
Police Academy movies, died in January 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.640 --> 00:32:26.800
her agency told The Hollywood Reporter.

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:27.840
The statement read,

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:31.040
"Marion carried with her a&nbsp;
kindness and permeating light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:35.040
that instantly filled a room upon her&nbsp;
arrival. The dimming of her light is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:39.040
already felt by those who knew her well.&nbsp;
We will miss her and always love her."

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:42.000
No cause of death was released. She was 73.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:47.760
Ramsey made her first appearance as Officer Hooks&nbsp;
in 1984's Police Academy after spending more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:52.560
a decade in the theatre world. She remained a&nbsp;
mainstay of the wacky comedy franchise over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:57.520
coming years, working on six installments.
 
 
Rapper and actor Dearon Thompson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:04.080
better known by stage his Deezer D, died of a&nbsp;
suspected heart attack in January 2021. Thompson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:09.920
who appeared as affable nurse Malik McGrath in&nbsp;
all 15 seasons of the beloved medical drama ER,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.920 --> 00:33:16.560
was discovered in an unresponsive state at his&nbsp;
Los Angeles home, his brother told TMZ. He was 55.

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:22.320
Thompson made his first feature film appearance&nbsp;
in 1991's Cool as Ice, the critically panned movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:29.840
debut of Vanilla Ice. He made his ER debut in 1994&nbsp;
beginning a near-200 episode run on the NBC hit.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:34.000
Thompson had been dealing with heart&nbsp;
issues for over a decade. In 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:39.760
he told RadarOnline that he was awaiting surgery&nbsp;
after, quote, "over 10 episodes of heart failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:44.560
in the last eight months alone." Doctors&nbsp;
identified a problem with his aorta. The Los&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:49.680
Angeles native was still making music and actively&nbsp;
promoting his songs at the time of his death.  
 
 &nbsp;

00:33:50.560 --> 00:33:54.640
British actress Barbara Shelley, who&nbsp;
appeared in numerous Hammer Horror films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.640 --> 00:34:02.240
during the 1950s and '60s, died in January 2021.&nbsp;
The veteran scream queen contracted COVID-19&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:08.320
after a routine hospital visit, her agent,&nbsp;
Thomas Bowington, told the BBC. She was 88.

00:34:08.320 --> 00:34:12.640
Shelley trained in theater as a child but her&nbsp;
good looks naturally led her into the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:16.960
modeling. Her dream was to act, however, so&nbsp;
when she was approached with an offer while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:22.080
vacationing in Rome, she took a chance. Shelley&nbsp;
went on to appear in a string of Italian movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:26.480
over a four-year period, but it would be the&nbsp;
work she did after returning to the UK that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:31.520
she would be best remembered for. The Londoner&nbsp;
became the go-to female star for Hammer Horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:34.080
appearing in eight of the&nbsp;
studio's classic pictures.

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:38.880
"Let me kiss you."
 
 
Beloved soap star John Reilly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:44.480
best known for his 11-year run on ABC's&nbsp;
General Hospital, died in January 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:48.960
The actor's death was confirmed by one of&nbsp;
his daughters in a tribute Instagram post.

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:49.840
She wrote,

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:54.000
"Imagine the best person in the world.&nbsp;
Now imagine that person being your dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:57.760
I'm so grateful he was mine. I'm&nbsp;
so grateful I got to love him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.760 --> 00:35:01.120
I'm so grateful I made it in time&nbsp;
to hold him and say goodbye."

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:01.840
He was 86.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:07.680
Reilly made his onscreen debut in the Midwest-set&nbsp;
soap opera As the World Turns in the mid-'70s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:11.760
and would go on to become a regular&nbsp;
face on American daytime television.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:16.160
He scored recurring roles on shows like&nbsp;
The Bionic Woman and How the West Was Won.

00:35:17.120 --> 00:35:21.680
Tanya Roberts was best known for playing&nbsp;
geologist Stacey Sutton opposite a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:28.160
older Roger Moore in 1985's A View to a Kill,&nbsp;
the 14th installment in the British James Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:33.120
franchise. The actress died from a urinary tract&nbsp;
infection that, quote, "got into her blood"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:38.800
and developed into sepsis, her longtime partner,&nbsp;
Lance O'Brien, told Fox News. She was 65.

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:42.160
The New York native started out as&nbsp;
a model but made the transition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:44.800
acting a decade before her James Bond appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:49.760
landing a part in the horror flick Forced&nbsp;
Entry. Her first major role came in 1980,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:54.320
when she joined the cast of the then-struggling&nbsp;
Charlie's Angels. She remained busy during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:59.600
1990s, when she made her first appearance&nbsp;
on That '70s Show; playing Midge Pinciotti.

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:05.680
Croatian-American actress Mira Furlan, best&nbsp;
known for her stints on Babylon 5 and Lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.680 --> 00:36:10.960
died in January 2021. Her death was&nbsp;
announced on her official Twitter account.

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:14.160
The cause of death was not revealed. She was 56.

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:19.040
Born in Zagreb, Furlan and her husband&nbsp;
emigrated to America in the early '90s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:23.040
amid rising tensions in their home&nbsp;
country, settling in New York City.

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:28.400
Three years after the move, she landed what would&nbsp;
become her most iconic role: Minbari Ambassador&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:34.000
Delenn in Babylon 5. She briefly returned to&nbsp;
Croatia before jetting off to Hawaii to play the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:40.080
part of Danielle Rousseau in Lost. She appeared as&nbsp;
the French loner in 20 episodes of ABC's hit show.

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:46.880
"You offered to fix my music box after all I've&nbsp;
done to you, striking you, shocking you."
 
 &nbsp;

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:52.640
New York-born actor Gregory Sierra, who&nbsp;
appeared in '70s sitcoms Sanford and Son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:56.560
and Barney Miller, died of cancer in January 2021.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:59.120
His widow, Helen Tabor, told CNN,

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:02.240
"He was doing the best he could&nbsp;
and just couldn't do it anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:06.400
He was quite wonderful, and my heart&nbsp;
is broken into 400 million pieces."

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:07.520
He was 83.

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:12.720
According to Deadline, Sierra began his career&nbsp;
as a member of the National Shakespeare Company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:18.400
appearing in a number of off-Broadway productions&nbsp;
during the 1960s. He made his screen debut in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:24.480
1969 episode of the Robert Wagner spy series, It&nbsp;
Takes a Thief. He would go on to become a mainstay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.480 --> 00:37:29.200
of the American TV in the years that followed,&nbsp;
popping up in numerous sitcoms and cop shows.
 
 &nbsp;

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:32.240
Award winning actress Cloris Leachman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:36.320
best known for playing Phyllis Lindstrom&nbsp;
on The Mary Tyler Moore Show and

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:38.560
“I am Frau Blücher “

00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:43.360
…died of natural causes in January&nbsp;
2021. She was, quote, "one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:48.640
most fearless actresses of our time," her rep&nbsp;
and friend, Juliet Green, told Variety, adding,

00:37:48.640 --> 00:37:51.200
"You never knew what Cloris&nbsp;
was going to say or do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:54.960
and that unpredictable quality was&nbsp;
part of her unparalleled magic."

00:37:54.960 --> 00:37:56.080
She was 94.

00:37:56.080 --> 00:38:00.160
A native of Iowa, Leachman moved to&nbsp;
neighboring Illinois to study theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:04.640
at Northwestern University. She dropped&nbsp;
out before she could graduate and took an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:10.320
unconventional route to Hollywood that included&nbsp;
an appearance at the 1946 Miss America pageant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:14.640
Her acting career began when she landed in&nbsp;
New York and got in with the Actors Studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:18.800
but she wouldn't come to national attention&nbsp;
until she debuted on The Mary Tyler Moore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:23.360
Show some 20 years later. She won two&nbsp;
of her eight primetime Emmy Awards for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.360 --> 00:38:27.920
playing the catty Phyllis Lindstrom, who&nbsp;
would later get her own spin-off, Phyllis.

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:29.200
"I'm afraid I've just been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:34.240
too much of a real woman."
 
 
Hollywood trailblazer Cicely Tyson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:38.640
who paved a path for Black actresses during&nbsp;
a career that spanned more than six decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.640 --> 00:38:43.920
died in January 2021, her manager&nbsp;
confirmed to Variety. She was 96.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:48.080
The East Harlem native worked as a model&nbsp;
before breaking into acting, appearing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:53.280
top publications like Vogue. She learned her craft&nbsp;
at the Actors Studio and became a regular face on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:57.760
the New York theater scene, but she'll no doubt be&nbsp;
best remembered for the work she did — and didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:03.600
do — on the screen. Tyson made her debut in short&nbsp;
drama Carib Gold and moved into feature films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:08.960
proper when she appeared as a jazz club worker in&nbsp;
the Harry Belafonte picture Odds Against Tomorrow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.960 --> 00:39:13.360
She largely avoided movies in the decade that&nbsp;
followed, making a conscious effort to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:13.360 --> 00:39:19.680
down roles that demeaned Black women. She returned&nbsp;
to the big screen with a bang in 1972's Sounder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:24.880
earning a Best Actress nomination for her turn&nbsp;
as Depression era sharecropper Rebecca Morgan.

00:39:24.880 --> 00:39:26.960
"You got you a low life job, Mr. Sheriff."

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:32.240
Tyson was awarded an honorary Oscar&nbsp;
in 2019, by which point she was widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.240 --> 00:39:38.320
recognized as one of the industry's pioneers. 
 
 
Actor Dustin Diamond, who played iconic '90s geek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:44.400
Samuel "Screech" Powers on the sitcom Saved&nbsp;
by the Bell, died of cancer in February 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:49.840
just three weeks after it was revealed that he'd&nbsp;
been diagnosed with stage 4 small cell carcinoma.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:52.880
In a statement published&nbsp;
in Variety, his agent said,

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:56.320
"In that time, it managed to spread&nbsp;
rapidly throughout his system;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.320 --> 00:40:01.760
the only mercy it exhibited was its sharp&nbsp;
and swift execution. Dustin did not suffer."

00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:03.040
He was 44.

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:08.480
A naturally funny child, Diamond landed the role&nbsp;
of Screech with next to no on-screen experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:13.280
He debuted as the loveable loser in 1988&nbsp;
on the Disney Channel's Good Morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.280 --> 00:40:18.000
Miss Bliss, which would be picked up and&nbsp;
revamped into Saved by the Bell by NBC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:22.880
when it was canned after a single season.&nbsp;
Saved by the Bell finished in 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:27.360
but Diamond reprised the role of Screech on&nbsp;
several occasions in the years that followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:31.360
most notably in Saved by the Bell: The&nbsp;
College Years and Saved by the Bell:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.360 --> 00:40:37.840
The New Class. The California native appeared in&nbsp;
130 episodes of The New Class from 1994 to 2000.
 
 &nbsp;

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:41.760
Award winning star of stage&nbsp;
and screen Hal Holbrook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:46.400
best known for his portrayal of Mark Twain in&nbsp;
his enduring one-man show Mark Twain Tonight!,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:46.400 --> 00:40:52.800
died at his California home in February 2021,&nbsp;
his agent told The New York Times. He was 95.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:58.240
His first on-stage performances took place when he&nbsp;
was stationed in Newfoundland with the U.S. Army;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.240 --> 00:41:03.120
he joined a theater group and appeared in a number&nbsp;
of local productions. He began working on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:09.120
would eventually become Mark Twain Tonight! in&nbsp;
1952, and four years later he shot to prominence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:09.120 --> 00:41:14.400
after Ed Sullivan invited him onto his variety&nbsp;
show. Remarkably, Holbrook would go on to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.400 --> 00:41:20.800
as Twain over 2,000 times, including two stints on&nbsp;
Broadway. The first run scored him a Tony Award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:24.880
and he was nominated for an Emmy when&nbsp;
Mark Twain Tonight! transferred to TV.
 
 &nbsp;

00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:28.080
Star of the stage and screen Christopher Plummer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:31.520
known for a prolific career that&nbsp;
included playing Captain von Trapp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:31.520 --> 00:41:38.240
in the iconic Best Picture winner The Sound&nbsp;
of Music, died in February 2021. He was 91.

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:44.720
The Toronto native made his Broadway bow in 1954's&nbsp;
The Starcross Story and debuted on the big screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:50.880
four years later, starring alongside Henry Fonda&nbsp;
and Susan Strasberg in Stage Struck. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.880 --> 00:41:56.160
disliked many of the films he appeared in during&nbsp;
the 1960s, including the one he would come to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.160 --> 00:42:01.360
best known for — He famously called Robert&nbsp;
Wise's musical, quote, "The Sound of Mucus".

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:06.720
In the 1970s, Plummer bagged a Tony Award&nbsp;
for his work on the musical Cyrano and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:12.400
a Primetime Emmy for The Moneychangers, NBC's&nbsp;
adaptation of Arthur Hailey's political thriller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:12.400 --> 00:42:16.560
He was nominated in the same category&nbsp;
for 2017's All the Money in the World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:20.080
a feat made all the more remarkable by&nbsp;
the fact that he stepped in to replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:25.200
Kevin Spacey in the role of J. Paul Getty just six&nbsp;
weeks before the film was due to be released.
 
 &nbsp;

00:42:26.080 --> 00:42:30.080
Actress and singer Trisha Noble, who&nbsp;
played the mother of Natalie Portman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:36.400
Padmé Amidala in the Star Wars universe, died&nbsp;
in January 2021. The Australian died, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:39.440
"after an 18-month battle with mesothelioma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:45.280
a tumour caused by inhaled asbestos fibers,"&nbsp;
the Daily Mail reported. She was 76.

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:50.080
Born Patsy Ann Noble in Marrickville, Sydney,&nbsp;
she got her start on the Aussie variety show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:55.280
Bandstand, which featured regular performances&nbsp;
from the likes of the Bee Gees and Grease star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.280 --> 00:43:00.000
Olivia Newton-John. Noble saw success in&nbsp;
Australia, but her career wouldn't take off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.760
internationally until she moved to Britain, where&nbsp;
she secured a record deal with Columbia Records.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:10.720
She later relocated again, taking the stage&nbsp;
name Trisha upon her arrival in the States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:15.200
She's perhaps best known for playing&nbsp;
Jobal Naberrie in 2005's Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:17.120
Episode III – Revenge of the Sith.
 
 &nbsp;

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:22.240
Actor and lifelong Buddhist Christopher&nbsp;
Pennock, who appeared in 126 episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.240 --> 00:43:28.000
of the Gothic soap opera Dark Shadows, and later&nbsp;
recurred on General Hospital, died in February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:35.600
2021. He had been given a melanoma diagnosis the&nbsp;
previous year, Deadline confirmed. He was 76.

00:43:35.600 --> 00:43:39.920
Pennock studied at the American Academy of&nbsp;
Dramatic Arts in New York and would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.920 --> 00:43:45.040
to finetune his craft with The Actors Studio&nbsp;
before making his debut on Broadway. He began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:49.600
with understudy work, but he quickly leveled&nbsp;
up and started landing roles in productions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:54.720
like 1969's Patriot for Me, which is&nbsp;
where Dan Curtis first laid eyes on him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:59.120
The Dark Shadows creator cast Pennock as&nbsp;
Leviathan, the first of many characters he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:04.400
would portray — he's perhaps best known for the&nbsp;
twin roles of Cyrus Longworth and John Yaeger, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:10.132
in-universe versions of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde.
 

